@import docs from '../../layouts/docs.clarus'
@import callout from '../../components/callout.clarus'

<docs [title]="'What is clarusjs?'" [path]="'/guide/what-is-clarusjs'" [description]="'clarusjs compiles templates to server HTML and ships JavaScript only for the interactive parts.'">
  <h1>What is clarusjs?</h1>
  <p class="lead">clarusjs is a compiled view framework built on Vite. You write components in a familiar template language; clarusjs turns them into plain server HTML and ships JavaScript only for the parts of a page that are interactive.</p>

  <h2>What makes it different</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HTML first.</strong> A component without a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> is HTML only, forever, at zero JavaScript cost.</li>
    <li><strong>No hydration.</strong> Interactive components (islands) attach to the HTML the server already sent. Nothing re-renders, so nothing can mismatch.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors at build time.</strong> Template mistakes fail the build with a file, line and column, and <code>clarusjs check</code> type-checks templates with TypeScript.</li>
    <li><strong>Readable output.</strong> The compiled JavaScript is short and plain. You can open it and follow it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to use it</h2>
  <p>clarusjs fits sites that are mostly content with interactive parts: documentation, marketing sites, blogs, shops, dashboards with a few live widgets. If every part of every screen is interactive (a design tool, a spreadsheet), a client-side framework is a better fit.</p>
  <callout [type]="'info'"><p>clarusjs is early (<code>0.x</code>). This site is its proving ground: every feature is built because the site needs it, and tested here in a real browser.</p></callout>
</docs>
